## Regression and Classification

Regression and classification comes down to one question: ***is the output a category, or a number?*** 

In the next levels you will see Anita's example for regression: 

- Regression example: "Based on today's temperature, how many customers should I expect?" That's a number. 

- Classification example: "Will this customer leave a positive review?" That's a category, yes or no.

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>&#128161; Teacher tip:</strong> the lesson deliberately starts classification with just two categories for simplicity, worth telling students explicitly that classification can involve more than two.</div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #D5EFFF; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin-top: 20px;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #06338D; line-height: 1.6;">
    <strong>&#8505; Watch for this during the warm-up:</strong> when students brainstorm uncertainties Anita faces, some will propose business decisions ("how much should we charge?") instead of genuine prediction questions. The fix is reframing: "How many people will buy ice cream if we charge $9 per scoop?" turns a decision into something a model could actually predict.
  </div>
</div>


